MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The owner's #1 finding, the camera half. Four changes, one seam:

- The right button is now a state machine (input.rs, pure, 14 tests):
  press-and-release under RIGHT_DRAG_COMMAND_MAX_PX (6 px) is the command
  click; travel beyond it becomes the Generals-style grab-and-drag map
  scroll, and the release then orders NOTHING. The press records, the
  release decides. A press whose ground point is off-screen (above the
  horizon) re-grabs at the threshold crossing, so a drag that starts on
  the sky still scrolls.
- Right-click ordering itself moves to the RELEASE: the context order
  (rally/attack/gather/move) fires on a sub-threshold release at the
  release position; a minimap right-click orders at the mapped point and
  now honors an armed-at-press attack-move (fixing the dead branch the
  §1.1 audit found — the old code disarmed before the minimap check read
  it); a right-click over the bottom-bar buttons is swallowed (UI is not
  a world order); right-press still cancels placement and consumes the
  whole gesture.
- Middle-drag now ROTATES the camera (Generals middle-drag; it panned
  before). Horizontal travel orbits at MIDDLE_DRAG_RAD_PER_PX, direction
  pinned to match E (orbit right) at yaw 0 and 90.
- Edge scrolling: the old 24 px binary full-speed zone becomes a 14 px
  band whose speed scales linearly with the cursor's depth into it —
  the owner's 'only the very last pixel works' finding was the snap.
  All four edges, corners combine, focused windows only, and a wired
  edge_scroll_enabled flag awaits the Phase 3 settings toggle.

Direction pinned at yaw 0 and yaw 90 for the grab-scroll (the world
follows the drag; camera-relative, not world-fixed) and the rotate.
Zero golden movement: presentation/input only.
